\details{
Blocking keys and comparison columns are written as ordinary R expressions
evaluated against the data, so a phonetic code, a substring or any other
transformation goes in the call itself rather than into a column prepared
beforehand:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{block = list(list(soundex(surname), dob), fsubstr(postcode, 1, 2))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The outer \code{list()} separates blocking \emph{passes}: a pair is a candidate if it
agrees on the key of any one pass, and each pair is reported once, with
\code{pass} recording the first pass that found it. Within a pass, \code{list()} or
\code{c()} separates the \emph{terms} joined into one key, so the pass above requires
the same Soundex of surname \emph{and} the same date of birth. Passes may have
different numbers of terms. A single expression, or a \code{c()} of column
names, is one pass.

Expressions see the data first and the calling environment behind it, so
\code{soundex(surname, nthreads = my_threads)} resolves \code{my_threads} from the
caller. \code{.data$x} and \code{.env$x} disambiguate a column that shares a name
with a local variable, and \verb{!!} builds a specification programmatically.
}
\section{Missing values}{

A record whose key is missing pairs with nothing, which is what makes
blocking on an incomplete variable safe. With the default
\code{match_missing = FALSE} a missing value in \emph{any} term makes the whole key
for that pass missing. \code{match_missing = TRUE} instead treats "missing" as a
value of its own, so two records both missing a term can pair on it --- the
behaviour of the \code{missingPos = FALSE} default in the function this
replaces. \code{na_strings} lists the text that counts as missing, \code{""} by
default; set it to \code{character()} to take blanks literally.
}

\section{Comparators}{

\code{compare} is a list of comparator calls whose first argument is the column
to compare. Any function of two character vectors works, so switching
metric is a one-word edit:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{compare = list(
    jaro_winkler(given_name),
    sn_jac = jaccard_index(surname, q = 3),
    name   = jaro_winkler_tokens(full_name, contractions = TRUE),
    exact  = (function(a, b) a == b)(postcode)
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A comparator of your own is applied to its column the same way, so it is
either named first (\code{my_cmp(surname)}) or written inline and applied, as
\code{exact} is above. A function on its own names no column and is an error.

A score column takes its name from the list element; unnamed elements fall
back to \verb{<column>_<comparator>}, so \code{jaro_winkler(given_name)} becomes
\code{given_name_jaro_winkler}. Write \code{column_1 | column_2} to compare columns
named differently in the two inputs. \code{nthreads} reaches a comparator only
when it declares that argument, so plain functions of two arguments are
safe to supply.

Comparators disagree about which end of their scale means "identical":
\code{\link[=jaro_winkler]{jaro_winkler()}} and the \verb{*_similarity()} family return 1, edit distances
such as \code{\link[=levenshtein]{levenshtein()}} and \code{\link[=hamming]{hamming()}} return 0, and the RapidFuzz
\verb{fuzz_*} scorers return 100. Each comparator is therefore probed once on a
calibration pair and, where needed, converted so that \strong{1 always means an
exact match} --- an unbounded edit distance by
\code{1 - distance / max(nchar(a), nchar(b))}, pairwise. \code{normalise = FALSE}
leaves every comparator's own scale untouched. What was detected is
recorded in \code{attr(out, "compare")} and printed when \code{verbose = TRUE}.
}

